1) Q60 changed data that should NOT have changed
   Your query:
     UPDATE products SET quantityInStock = quantityInStock - 10 WHERE productLine != 'Electric cars';
   What's wrong:
     "!=" means "NOT equal". This reduced the stock of every OLD product (about 110 rows)
     and did not touch your two new products at all, which is the opposite of what you wanted.
   Why it matters:
     In a real system this would corrupt live stock data. This is exactly why the rule is:
     run a SELECT with the same WHERE first and check the row count before any UPDATE/DELETE.
   Action:
     Restore the database (re-import mysqlsampledatabase.sql or your backup), then redo Part 6.
   Correct version (after adding the order lines in Q56):
     UPDATE products p
     JOIN orderdetails od ON od.productCode = p.productCode
     SET p.quantityInStock = p.quantityInStock - od.quantityOrdered
     WHERE od.orderNumber = 555555;

2) Comma joins with no join condition (Q36, Q48, Q49)
   Example from Q36:
     FROM orderdetails, products, orders WHERE orders.orderNumber = 10100
   What's wrong:
     You listed tables with commas but never told SQL how they connect.
   Why:
     Without a join condition, SQL makes a CARTESIAN PRODUCT: every row of one table is
     combined with every row of the other. That produces thousands of wrong rows.
   Rule:
     Always use an explicit JOIN ... ON ... between tables. Never use commas.

3) LEFT JOIN direction (Q33, Q37, Q38, Q39)
   Rule:
     LEFT JOIN keeps ALL rows from the table on the LEFT (the one after FROM).
     Put the table you want to keep completely on the left.
   Example:
     "All customers, including those with 0 orders" → FROM customers LEFT JOIN orders
     NOT FROM orders LEFT JOIN customers.


